WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.169
[suspense music]

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:13.138
[suspense music]

00:00:18.935 --> 00:00:22.689
(host)
Some monsters call to
mind very specific images.

00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:25.442
Their iconic onscreen
personas overshadow

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.318
their earlier histories.

00:00:27.402 --> 00:00:30.613
I'm talking about Frankenstein
and his creature, Dracula;

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.783
the Invisible Man,
the Wolf Man, the Mummy.

00:00:34.659 --> 00:00:36.161
Why is this?

00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:37.245
Universal Pictures.

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:41.374
These famous Universal
monster faces

00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:44.335
inspired decades of
Halloween costumes

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:46.296
and make up a distinctive
brand of horror

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.965
that defined early
Hollywood cinema.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:52.010
With strategic decisions in
wardrobe and special effects,

00:00:52.093 --> 00:00:54.971
uniform marketing,
innovative cinematography,

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:58.475
and casting that turned
everyday actors into stars,

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.810
Universal Pictures
forever changed the game

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:03.146
for onscreen monsters.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.940
[suspense music]

00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:14.532
At the turn of the 20th century,

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:16.493
early silent horror
films were largely

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:20.455
macabre European creations
featuring ghost skeletons

00:01:20.538 --> 00:01:23.500
and wizards set against
gothic backdrops.

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:27.212
The 1922 German film "Nosferatu"
was a fitting fulfillment

00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:29.881
of the Eastern European
vampire's legacy

00:01:29.964 --> 00:01:33.259
and the first horror film
with an original score.

00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:35.929
[ominous music]

00:01:40.558 --> 00:01:42.977
But what is now considered
to be the canonical period

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:46.606
of horror cinema didn't come
about until Universal emerged

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:49.359
as a powerhouse
motion picture studio.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:53.071
Founded in 1912, Universal
Film Manufacturing Company

00:01:53.154 --> 00:01:56.115
initially saw critical
and financial success

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:57.450
but like most of America,

00:01:57.534 --> 00:01:59.369
struggled during the
Great Depression.

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.079
The solution to
the studio's woes

00:02:01.162 --> 00:02:04.499
came in an unexpected
form, monsters.

00:02:04.582 --> 00:02:07.836
Universal's 1930s business
strategy started with a plan

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:10.547
to make movies on as
tight a budget as possible,

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.632
and horror fit the bill nicely.

00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:15.510
The elements of gothic
storytelling combined swimmingly

00:02:15.593 --> 00:02:18.054
with the low lighting and
imaginative camera tricks

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:20.849
that disguised budget
conscious sets.

00:02:20.932 --> 00:02:22.433
Unknown actors
could be concealed

00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:24.769
behind creative makeup effects,
and hiring

00:02:24.853 --> 00:02:28.189
veteran European crew members
familiar with gothic work

00:02:28.273 --> 00:02:31.693
made adapting spooky stories
for Hollywood easier.

00:02:31.776 --> 00:02:34.612
With the horrors of World War
I and the Great Depression,

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:39.158
America adopted isolationists
policies alongside patriotism.

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:41.786
The monstrous figure
embodied outside threats

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:45.248
to these modern constructions,
literally entering the homes

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:48.626
of families to tear them
down from the inside.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:49.544
[monster roars]

00:02:49.627 --> 00:02:52.797
[Elizabeth screaming]

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:58.261
Other common themes in
Universal's golden age

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.097
of horror films included
the mad scientist hell bent

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.475
on playing God, the crumbling
ancestral home or castle,

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:06.853
class tensions,
failed marriages,

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:09.689
and challenges to
traditional gender roles,

00:03:09.772 --> 00:03:11.983
all amidst a gothic setting
that alluded

00:03:12.066 --> 00:03:15.194
to the historical but
evoked present day fears--

00:03:15.278 --> 00:03:16.905
a clever way for
a studio to appeal

00:03:16.988 --> 00:03:19.616
to what's already in
the minds of the people.

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:23.286
Universal's first venture
into horror began in 1931

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:27.165
with the infamous undead
blood sucker himself, Dracula,

00:03:27.248 --> 00:03:30.752
an established character
in folklore and literature.

00:03:30.835 --> 00:03:34.631
Both "Dracula" and "Nosferatu"
predate Universal's version

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:37.926
but Universal's "Dracula" was
the first to legally adapt

00:03:38.009 --> 00:03:40.303
Bram Stoker's famous text.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.305
After purchasing the
rights to both the novel

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:45.767
and theater adaptation
of "Dracula" in 1929,

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:49.687
studio executive Carl Laemmle,
Jr. green lit the film version

00:03:49.771 --> 00:03:52.565
with a few caveats,
a tight budget,

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:55.151
prioritization of elements
from the stage play

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:58.905
and a short enough runtime
to use in a double feature.

00:03:58.988 --> 00:04:01.616
Tod Browning was hired as
director and Charles D. Hall

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:03.451
was tapped for set design,

00:04:03.534 --> 00:04:06.287
but the studio still
needed the star.

00:04:06.371 --> 00:04:08.831
While other more famous
actors were considered,

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:12.043
Bela Lugosi stood out
as the logical choice.

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:15.213
He had played Dracula on
Broadway and he was cheap,

00:04:15.296 --> 00:04:17.590
and thank the fates for that.

00:04:17.674 --> 00:04:20.426
Lugosi's portrayal of the
count in Universal's makeup

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:23.596
and wardrobe became the most
influential visual portrayal

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.224
of vampires to this day.

00:04:26.307 --> 00:04:30.311
The pale skin, dark, slick back
hair, aristocratic wardrobe,

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:33.356
an infamous cape, became
the iconic depiction

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:36.025
we frequently
associate with Dracula.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:38.820
And even though many modern
interpretations of vampires

00:04:38.903 --> 00:04:40.613
have moved past this portrayal,

00:04:40.697 --> 00:04:43.658
the countless imitations
of the Universal brand,

00:04:43.741 --> 00:04:45.660
not to mention the
Halloween costumes,

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:47.996
made this vampire an icon.

00:04:49.247 --> 00:04:50.790
After the success of "Dracula",

00:04:50.873 --> 00:04:53.835
Universal was thirsty
for another home run.

00:04:53.918 --> 00:04:56.087
They offered prominent
director James Whale

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:59.090
a slew of different project
concepts to choose from

00:04:59.173 --> 00:05:01.634
and he went with another
monster property,

00:05:01.718 --> 00:05:03.803
Mary Shelley's "Frankenstein".

00:05:03.886 --> 00:05:06.681
Like "Dracula" before it,
the movie success hinged

00:05:06.764 --> 00:05:09.058
on the commercial
appeal of the monster.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:11.519
Whale insisted on
casting Boris Karloff,

00:05:11.602 --> 00:05:13.813
a noted but under-recognized
character actor

00:05:13.896 --> 00:05:15.314
as the creature.

00:05:15.398 --> 00:05:17.775
The neck bolts,
the exaggerated brow

00:05:17.859 --> 00:05:19.736
and the suit were
a notable departure

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:22.071
from Mary Shelley's
original creation.

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.824
Yet the emotion that Shelley
wrote into the character

00:05:24.907 --> 00:05:27.160
was still present
thanks to Karloff.

00:05:27.243 --> 00:05:29.912
Universal's interpretation
would define both the actor

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:33.332
and Shelley's character
into the 21st century.

00:05:33.416 --> 00:05:35.543
Take care, now, Frankenstein!
Take care.

00:05:37.462 --> 00:05:40.298
(host)
And in the "Frankenstein"
film's final iconic face-off

00:05:40.381 --> 00:05:42.675
between
Henry and his creature,

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:45.720
Universal continued to use
innovative cinematography

00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:47.472
as a hallmark of the genre.

00:05:47.555 --> 00:05:49.891
When the two gaze at each other
through the spinning wheel

00:05:49.974 --> 00:05:53.603
of the windmill, the slats'
broken revelation of their faces

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:57.690
mimicking a film projector and
its precursor, the zoetrope.

00:05:57.774 --> 00:06:00.902
[monster roars]

00:06:00.985 --> 00:06:04.030
"Frankenstein" was the first
movie promoted by a studio

00:06:04.113 --> 00:06:07.283
as a horror picture and
that decision was not met

00:06:07.366 --> 00:06:08.743
without resistance,

00:06:08.826 --> 00:06:11.496
primarily from Catholic
and conservative leaders.

00:06:11.579 --> 00:06:14.165
Critics condemned a
controversial hanging scene

00:06:14.248 --> 00:06:16.918
and some of the film's
dialogue as heretical.

00:06:17.001 --> 00:06:18.669
Trying to address
these concerns,

00:06:18.753 --> 00:06:22.173
Universal included a unique
prologue to the feature

00:06:22.256 --> 00:06:25.009
condemning Frankenstein's
actions and warning the audience

00:06:25.093 --> 00:06:26.636
of what they will see.

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:30.014
We are about to unfold
the story of Frankenstein,

00:06:30.098 --> 00:06:33.851
a man of science who
sought to create a man

00:06:33.935 --> 00:06:38.856
after his own image
without reckoning upon God.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.568
(host)
Still, some newspapers refused
to publish ads for the film,

00:06:42.652 --> 00:06:45.154
and censors demanded edits.

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:48.574
But the saying, any publicity
is good publicity worked here.

00:06:48.658 --> 00:06:50.993
Made for less than $300,000,

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:53.663
"Frankenstein"
outperformed "Dracula,"

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:56.916
grossing $12
million domestically,

00:06:56.999 --> 00:07:00.753
and Universal had another
successful monster.

00:07:00.837 --> 00:07:04.757
In 1932, Universal drew
inspiration from recent events--

00:07:04.841 --> 00:07:07.635
the discovery of the
tomb of King Tutankhamun.

00:07:07.718 --> 00:07:11.430
His shrunken mummified corpse
captured the world's attention,

00:07:11.514 --> 00:07:14.308
as did the rumored
accompanying curse.

00:07:14.392 --> 00:07:16.769
"The Mummy" told
a tale of forbidden love

00:07:16.853 --> 00:07:18.980
using the now
recognizable Boris Karloff

00:07:19.063 --> 00:07:20.815
as a titular character.

00:07:20.898 --> 00:07:24.569
With a powerful score and
clever shots of miniature sets,

00:07:24.652 --> 00:07:27.697
viewers were transported
to an exotic interpretation

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.867
of Egypt that paid homage
to the undercurrent of magic

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:34.662
that fueled so many of film's
earliest gothic stories.

00:07:34.745 --> 00:07:36.581
Like its Universal precursors,

00:07:36.664 --> 00:07:39.959
"The Mummy" cinematic creativity
significantly contributed

00:07:40.042 --> 00:07:42.003
to its historical permanence.

00:07:42.086 --> 00:07:44.881
Pioneering cameraman Carl
"Papa" Freund moved the camera

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:47.341
in ways previously unseen.

00:07:47.425 --> 00:07:50.803
He freed the camera from
the typical static tripod,

00:07:50.887 --> 00:07:53.389
strapping it to his chest
for resting angles

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:56.267
and placing it on a crane
for expansive frames.

00:07:57.977 --> 00:08:02.356
In 1934, Hollywood instituted
the self-imposed Hays Code,

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.151
responding to industry
scandals of the 1920s

00:08:05.234 --> 00:08:08.029
and increasingly
boundary-challenging films.

00:08:08.112 --> 00:08:10.281
These censorship
guidelines aimed to curtail

00:08:10.364 --> 00:08:13.701
anything that didn't depict
the correct standards of life

00:08:13.784 --> 00:08:16.746
as defined by the rich men
in charge at the time.

00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:19.415
As a result, horror
didn't disappear on screen

00:08:19.498 --> 00:08:21.375
but became a bit more subdued.

00:08:21.459 --> 00:08:23.878
Universal, in spite of
the Hays Code, however,

00:08:23.961 --> 00:08:26.297
still marketed
their films as shocking,

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:28.674
meant to astound and horrify,

00:08:28.758 --> 00:08:31.135
which continued to
draw in audiences.

00:08:31.219 --> 00:08:33.304
Universal went on to
add "The Invisible Man"

00:08:33.387 --> 00:08:35.932
and "The Werewolf of London"
to their horror lineup,

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:38.392
bringing striking and
memorable special effects

00:08:38.476 --> 00:08:39.644
to the screen--

00:08:39.727 --> 00:08:42.647
not to mention two
more iconic monsters.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:44.023
The monster genre couldn't solve

00:08:44.106 --> 00:08:46.567
all of Universal's
financial struggles.

00:08:46.651 --> 00:08:48.736
Burdened with debt,
Universal was bought out

00:08:48.819 --> 00:08:51.489
by the Standard
Capital Bank in 1936,

00:08:51.572 --> 00:08:54.450
and the existing
studio heads were canned.

00:08:54.533 --> 00:08:57.078
New execs cut budgets
further, prioritizing musicals

00:08:57.161 --> 00:09:01.082
and halting production
on new horror content,

00:09:01.165 --> 00:09:03.000
leading to
an exciting development--

00:09:03.084 --> 00:09:05.753
the introduction of
the horror sequel.

00:09:05.836 --> 00:09:08.923
Eager to capitalize on their
earlier successes in the genre,

00:09:09.006 --> 00:09:12.051
Universal produced
a whole host of sequels.

00:09:12.134 --> 00:09:15.263
"Bride of Frankenstein,"
"Dracula's Daughter,"

00:09:15.346 --> 00:09:18.808
"Son of Frankenstein,"
"The Invisible Man Returns,"

00:09:18.891 --> 00:09:23.396
"The Mummy's Hand," "The Mummy's
Tomb," and "Son of Dracula."

00:09:23.479 --> 00:09:26.607
These burgeoning franchises
were financially strategic

00:09:26.691 --> 00:09:29.360
and further established
Universal's looming presence

00:09:29.443 --> 00:09:30.987
in horror cinema.

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.073
Using a now established cast
and crew from makeup artists

00:09:34.156 --> 00:09:37.451
to set designers, the visual
style of the Universal

00:09:37.535 --> 00:09:41.747
horror film stayed consistent
and immediately recognizable.

00:09:41.831 --> 00:09:44.792
Repeated visual and technical
cues like violent storms,

00:09:44.875 --> 00:09:48.587
operating room or lab sets,
offscreen female screams,

00:09:48.671 --> 00:09:51.090
large ancestral homes,
heavy use of shadows,

00:09:51.173 --> 00:09:55.094
and creative cinematic
framework solidified the style.

00:09:55.177 --> 00:09:57.722
Also in the 1940s,
Universal introduced

00:09:57.805 --> 00:10:00.599
back-to-back crossover films
to the industry--

00:10:00.683 --> 00:10:03.561
"Frankenstein Meets the Wolf
Man," "House of Frankenstein,"

00:10:03.644 --> 00:10:05.313
and "House of Dracula."

00:10:05.396 --> 00:10:07.440
These in-universe
collaborations

00:10:07.523 --> 00:10:10.526
put the recognizable figures
of Frankenstein's creature,

00:10:10.609 --> 00:10:11.944
Dracula, the Wolf Man,

00:10:12.028 --> 00:10:14.280
the Mad Doctor
and the Hunchback assistant

00:10:14.363 --> 00:10:15.948
together on screen,
like an Avengers-style universe

00:10:16.032 --> 00:10:20.161
with fewer rules
and no shared history.

00:10:20.244 --> 00:10:22.913
In the years following the
conclusion of World War II,

00:10:22.997 --> 00:10:25.458
movie-going audiences seemed
to lose their appetite

00:10:25.541 --> 00:10:27.001
for monster movies.

00:10:27.084 --> 00:10:30.004
Perhaps needing a break from
violence and horror in general,

00:10:30.087 --> 00:10:33.758
they opted for more uplifting
content at the box office.

00:10:33.841 --> 00:10:36.385
The monster movies that
were made during this period

00:10:36.469 --> 00:10:38.137
backed away from horror elements

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.432
and leaned into the comedic
potential of the genre.

00:10:41.515 --> 00:10:43.684
In the 1950s,
while Universal Studios,

00:10:43.768 --> 00:10:45.478
now Universal International,

00:10:45.561 --> 00:10:47.480
continued to produce
horror films,

00:10:47.563 --> 00:10:50.816
they prioritized science fiction
in response to world events

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:53.069
and audiences' shifting favor.

00:10:53.152 --> 00:10:56.322
While the quality of
these films is debated,

00:10:56.405 --> 00:10:58.824
there is little denying
that the decade contributed

00:10:58.908 --> 00:11:02.620
at least one more unique and
iconic universal monster,

00:11:03.746 --> 00:11:05.414
the "Creature from
the Black Lagoon."

00:11:06.582 --> 00:11:08.167
They marketed the film
as the strangest

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:10.586
science fiction thriller
of them all,

00:11:10.669 --> 00:11:12.671
hoping to attract
audiences enthralled

00:11:12.755 --> 00:11:15.508
with the newly popular genre
and who were comfortable

00:11:15.591 --> 00:11:18.677
with monster stories from
the previous two decades.

00:11:18.761 --> 00:11:21.222
This monster isn't based
so much on existing lore

00:11:21.305 --> 00:11:24.517
but does rely on a familiar
monster-as-human trope.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:27.603
Set in the Amazon, the
half-fish, half-human monster

00:11:27.686 --> 00:11:29.563
struggles with
very human feelings

00:11:29.647 --> 00:11:31.607
of isolation and loneliness.

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:39.865
Called the Gill Man, the
notable and beautifully designed

00:11:39.949 --> 00:11:42.493
character suit and
sophisticated makeup,

00:11:42.576 --> 00:11:44.328
under the direction
of noted actress

00:11:44.412 --> 00:11:46.997
and special effects
artist Milicent Patrick,

00:11:47.081 --> 00:11:50.459
stand up as a hallmark
of Universal's monsters.

00:11:50.543 --> 00:11:53.546
The film was also notable for
its underwater photography

00:11:53.629 --> 00:11:55.714
not common for the time.

00:11:55.798 --> 00:11:58.717
[suspense music]

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:04.432
It's not only a story that
makes a monster iconic.

00:12:04.515 --> 00:12:06.100
All the creators who contributed

00:12:06.183 --> 00:12:08.477
to Universal's classic
monsters did so

00:12:08.561 --> 00:12:10.396
with a specific intention.

00:12:10.479 --> 00:12:13.566
The writers, actors,
directors, cinematographers,

00:12:13.649 --> 00:12:15.901
marketing executives,
special effects teams,

00:12:15.985 --> 00:12:18.904
and costuming departments--
they formed the beating

00:12:18.988 --> 00:12:22.950
or undead heart of these
silver screen interpretations.

00:12:23.033 --> 00:12:25.119
I can't help but wonder
if the commercial

00:12:25.202 --> 00:12:27.580
and critical failure of
some of the reboot attempts

00:12:27.663 --> 00:12:29.707
is because they strayed too far

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:32.543
from the original
Universal monster formula.

00:12:32.626 --> 00:12:34.587
Take 2017's "The Mummy"--

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:36.839
big-name,
A-list Hollywood actors,

00:12:36.922 --> 00:12:38.799
huge budgets,
too heavy a reliance

00:12:38.883 --> 00:12:40.551
on digital special effects,

00:12:40.634 --> 00:12:43.596
and an obvious eye
on franchise tie-ins,

00:12:43.679 --> 00:12:46.515
it neglected to honor the
monster and its meaning.

00:12:49.435 --> 00:12:52.021
In my opinion, the magic
of the original films

00:12:52.104 --> 00:12:54.482
comes from the forced
creativity necessary

00:12:54.565 --> 00:12:56.942
of a smaller budget,
the gothic themes,

00:12:57.026 --> 00:13:00.070
and letting the monster
and its story sell the film.

00:13:05.659 --> 00:13:08.746
2020's "The Invisible Man"
came closer to this formula.

00:13:08.829 --> 00:13:12.249
With a $7 million budget,
a fraction of "The Mummy"'s,

00:13:12.333 --> 00:13:15.669
it uses the absence of the
monster's form--literally--

00:13:15.753 --> 00:13:17.713
in favor of atmospheric tension

00:13:17.796 --> 00:13:20.549
and some pretty horrifying
practical effects.

00:13:20.633 --> 00:13:24.386
Universal's monsters pushed
back against societal norms,

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:27.223
often embracing the taboos
that rigidly defined

00:13:27.306 --> 00:13:29.934
conventional American
social structures of family,

00:13:30.017 --> 00:13:32.311
patriarchy, gender, and class.

00:13:32.394 --> 00:13:34.855
While the golden age of
Universal classic monsters

00:13:34.939 --> 00:13:39.193
is long gone, its legacy
has become monstrous itself,

00:13:39.276 --> 00:13:41.570
enduring, frightening
and always there

00:13:41.654 --> 00:13:44.782
to scare our
imaginations into action.

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:49.620
Ahoy, matey, the newest
show over on PBS Origins

00:13:49.703 --> 00:13:51.747
will take you on
an enlightening voyage

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:54.208
through the pirate history
you've been missing.

00:13:54.291 --> 00:13:56.961
"Rogue History" unravels
historical myths,

00:13:57.044 --> 00:14:00.548
unearths lost narratives,
and champions unsung heroes.

00:14:00.631 --> 00:14:02.967
It's the pirate history
you were never told.

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:05.427
Hit the link in the
description and explore it now.

00:14:07.179 --> 00:14:08.264
- (speaker)Rolling?
- (host) Yeah.

00:14:08.347 --> 00:14:09.390
(speaker) And action.

00:14:13.102 --> 00:14:13.936
- Oh, no.

00:14:14.019 --> 00:14:14.853
Oh, God. [laughs]

00:14:14.937 --> 00:14:15.771
(speaker)
And action.

00:14:15.854 --> 00:14:18.691
[host laughing]

00:14:21.277 --> 00:14:22.278
I'm terrible.

00:14:22.361 --> 00:14:23.571
- No!
- That was amazing.

